king immediate-release metformin 500 mg twice daily orally for at least several days were studied. Metformin was undetectable (<5 mcg/L) in the serum of the 2 infants (extent of nursing not stated) aged 2 and 14 months in whom it was measured.[2]","Effects in Breastfed Infants":"Seven infants aged 5 to 25 months whose mothers were taking metformin (start date and duration not stated) were judged to be healthy with growth and development progressing as expected. Two of the infants also had normal Denver Developmental Screening tests.[1] Three infants aged 2, 5 and 14 months whose mothers were taking metformin 500 mg twice daily had no detectable adverse effects from metformin.[3] In 3 breastfed (extent not stated) infants aged 10 to 11 days postpartum whose mothers were taking an average metformin dosage of 9.6 mg/kg (range 7.5 to 12.4 mg/kg) daily, none of the infants had low blood glucose levels. Their mothers reported no adverse reactions in the infants.[4] Ninety-two mothers of 111 infants were treated with metformin in a mean dosage of 2.2 grams daily (range 1.5 to 2.55 mg daily) throughout pregnancy and postpartum. A 6-month, nonrandomized, prospective trial followed 61 predominantly breastfed and 50 formula-fed infants of these women. No differences in 3- and 6-month outcomes were found by blinded observers between the 2 groups of infants in height, weight, motor-social development or rates of illness.[6]","Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk":"In a retrospective study of 250 women who received metformin 500 mg to 2 grams daily in either the immediate- or extended-release formulation for polycystic ovary syndrome, information on breastfeeding was available on 164 women. Of these, 97 (59%) were successful at breastfeeding, 27 (17%) failed, and 40 (27%) made no attempt to breastfeed. Of the 124 who attempted to breastfeed, 78% were successful. Failures were attributed to poor milk production in 4 women, demands of multiple births, infant prematurity, cleft palate and mastitis. Most of the women stopped metformin by the 12th week of pregnancy.[7] In a follow-up to a placebo-controlled study on metformin use during pregnancy in women with polycystic ovary syndrome, women were asked about the duration and extent of breastfeeding. No difference in breastfeeding in the duration of exclusive or partial breastfeeding was observed between the women who received metformin during pregnancy and those who received placebo.[8] A small pilot study of women with low milk supply and at least one sign of insulin resistance were randomized to receive metformin (n = 10) or placebo (n = 5). Metformin (Glucophage XR) was given in doses that increased at weekly intervals from 750 mg to 1.5 grams to 2 grams daily. Milk output was determined by weighing their infants before and after feeding plus the weight of any pumped milk. At 2 to 4 weeks of the study, women given placebo had a reduction of 58 mL daily while those who received metformin increased their milk output by 8 mL daily; however this difference was not statistically significant.[9] A retrospective cohort study in Norway compared women with polycystic ovary disease (PCOS) who received metformin (n = 653) to women in a large cohort who did not have PCOS. The study showed no difference in exclusive breastfeeding between metformin- and placebo-treated women with PCOS. Hypertensive disorders and gestational diabetes did not affect the exclusive breastfeeding rate in PCOS.[10]"},"sections_missing":[],"truncated":false,"exposure_measurements":{"status":"quantitative_concentrations_extracted","concentration_passages":[{"concentration_values_as_reported":["270 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"Six women had milk levels that varied little over 24 hours and averaged 270 mcg/L.","preceding_context":"Six of the women were taking an immediate-release product 500 mg three times daily and one was taking 500 mg as a sustained-release product once daily.","source_section":"Drug Levels"},{"concentration_values_as_reported":["380 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"One woman had a slight rise in the milk level to about 380 mcg/L at about 4 to 5 hours after the dose.","preceding_context":"Six women had milk levels that varied little over 24 hours and averaged 270 mcg/L.","source_section":"Drug Levels"},{"concentration_values_as_reported":["270 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"Milk levels were relatively constant over the next 72 hours with average levels of 130 and 270 mcg/L; a slight peak occurred in one mother 12 hours after the dose.","preceding_context":"Two lactating women who were 14 and 14.5 months postpartum took a single dose of 500 mg of immediate-release metformin.","source_section":"Drug Levels"},{"concentration_values_as_reported":["130 to 270 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"Relatively stable milk levels averaging 130 to 270 mcg/L in the 3 mothers was found.","preceding_context":"In the same paper, 3 women who had been taking immediate-release metformin 500 mg twice daily orally for at least several days were studied.","source_section":"Drug Levels"},{"concentration_values_as_reported":["410 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"The average milk metformin levels were estimated to be 410 mcg/L.","preceding_context":"Trough and peak (2 hours after the dose) milk levels were collected 4 to 17 days postpartum.","source_section":"Drug Levels"},{"concentration_values_as_reported":["<10 mcg/L","80 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"In 2, metformin was undetectable (<10 mcg/L) 2.5 hours after the maternal dose, while in the other 2, levels of 50 and 80 mcg/L (10 and 15% of their mother’s serum levels) were found at 6 and 5.3 hours after the maternal dose, respectively.","preceding_context":"Serum levels were measured in 4 breastfed infants (ages and extent of nursing not stated) whose mothers were taking a median metformin dosage of 1.5 grams daily.","source_section":"Drug Levels"},{"concentration_values_as_reported":["<5 mcg/L"],"source_passage":"Metformin was undetectable (<5 mcg/L) in the serum of the 2 infants (extent of nursing not stated) aged 2 and 14 months in whom it was measured.[2]","preceding_context":"Three women 2, 5 and 14 months postpartum who had been taking immediate-release metformin 500 mg twice daily orally for at least several days were studied.","source_section":"Drug Levels"}],"additional_passages_omitted":false,"infant_level_excerpt":"Serum levels were measured in 4 breastfed infants (ages and extent of nursing not stated) whose mothers were taking a median metformin dosage of 1.5 grams daily. In 2, metformin was undetectable (<10 mcg/L) 2.5 hours after the maternal dose, while in the other 2, levels of 50 and 80 mcg/L (10 and 15% of their mother’s serum levels) were found at 6 and 5.3 hours after the maternal dose, respectively.[1,2] Three women 2, 5 and 14 months postpartum who had been taking immediate-release metformin 500 mg twice daily orally for at least several days were studied. Metformin was undetectable (<5 mcg/L) in the serum of the 2 infants (extent of nursing not stated) aged 2 and 14 months in whom it was measured.[2]","infant_level_section_present_in_returned_text":true,"exhaustive":false,"extraction_scope":"Exact concentration expressions from the returned Drug Levels excerpt only. No unit conversion, dose calculation or automatic specimen classification. Read the source passage and preceding context to distinguish milk, maternal serum and infant serum. Empty extraction is not proof that no measurements exist.","presentation_requirement":"Include a Measured exposure row in a summary of this record. Report the retrieved milk concentration values and units with their study context, separately from infant outcomes and administered regimens. Do not replace retrieved numeric concentrations with only low or trace. State infant-level availability or the missing excerpt. If measurements are omitted, explicitly disclose that omission. These are research measurements, not dosing instructions; never convert them to a personal dose."},"references":[{"number":"1","citation":"Hale TW, Kristensen JH, Hackett LP, et al. Transfer of metformin into human milk. Diabetologia 2002;45:1509-14.","pmid":"12436333","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12436333/"},{"number":"2","citation":"Hale T, Kristensen J, Hackett L, et al. Transfer of metformin into human milk. Adv Exp Med Biol 2004;554:435-6.","pmid":"15384618","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15384618/"},{"number":"3","citation":"Gardiner SJ, Kirkpatrick CM, Begg EJ, et al. Transfer of metformin into human milk. Clin Pharmacol Ther 2003;73:71-7.","pmid":"12545145","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12545145/"},{"number":"4","citation":"Briggs GG, Ambrose PJ, Nageotte MP, et al. Excretion of metformin into breast milk and the effect on nursing infants. Obstet Gynecol 2005;105:1437-41.","pmid":"15932841","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15932841/"},{"number":"5","citation":"Eyal S, Easterling TR, Carr D, et al. Pharmacokinetics of metformin during pregnancy. Drug Metab Dispos 2010;38:833-40.","pmid":"20118196","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20118196/"},{"number":"6","citation":"Glueck CJ, Salehi M, Sieve L, et al. Growth, motor, and social development in breast- and formula-fed infants of metformin-treated women with polycystic ovary syndrome. J Pediatr 2006;148:628-32.","pmid":"16737874","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16737874/"},{"number":"7","citation":"Thatcher SS, Jackson EM. Pregnancy outcome in infertile patients with polycystic ovary syndrome who were treated with metformin. Fertil Steril 2006;85:1002-9.","pmid":"16580387","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16580387/"},{"number":"8","citation":"Vanky E, Nordskar J, Leithe H, et al. Breast size increment during pregnancy and breastfeeding in mothers with polycystic ovary syndrome: A follow-up study of a randomised controlled trial on metformin versus placebo. BJOG 2012;119:1403-9.","pmid":"22827167","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22827167/"},{"number":"9","citation":"Nommsen-Rivers L, Thompson A, Riddle S, et al. Feasibility and acceptability of metformin to augment low milk supply: A pilot randomized controlled trial. J Hum Lact 2019;35:261-71.","pmid":"30629889","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30629889/"},{"number":"10","citation":"Husby AE, Simpson MR, Dalbye R, et al. Exclusive breastfeeding among women with polycystic ovary syndrome versus women from a population-based cohort: A cohort study. Int Breastfeed J 2026;21:54.","pmid":"42010413","doi":null,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42010413/"}],"record_revised_on":"2026-08-15","data_freshness":{"dataset":"LactMed","mode":"saved_copy_of_official_package","package_published_on":"2026-10-22","record_revised_on":"2026-08-15","age_days":32,"status":"within_check_window","check_after_days":60,"how_to_check":"Compare with the LactMed entry in https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pub/litarch/file_list.csv or open the record link.","display_note":"LactMed text comes from NCBI's official package published 2026-10-22; this record was last revised 2026-08-15. LactMed updates monthly, so check the record link for newer changes."},"matched_record":{"id":"4568984","title":"Metformin","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501020/"},"source":"snapshot:lactmed","sections_found":["Summary of Use during Lactation","Drug Levels","Effects in Breastfed Infants","Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk"],"sections_expected":["Summary of Use during Lactation","Drug Levels","Effects in Breastfed Infants","Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk"],"section_allocation":"Available sections share the text limit; unused space is redistributed. Each section retains its truncation status.","interpretation_contract":{"measurements":"Preserve relevant measured milk or infant-serum concentrations and their units separately from outcomes. These are research measurements, not administered doses or dos