rence_count_is_not_study_count":true,"no_effects_statement_detected":true,"interpretation":"A no-effects statement does not establish safety or a well-studied population. Study counts and designs need source/clinical review; missing counts are unknown, not zero."},"source_balance_context":{"text":"Data from well-conducted studies indicate that metformin levels in milk are low and infants would receive less than 0.5% of their mother’s weight-adjusted dosage. Milk metformin levels are relatively constant during maternal metformin use, so timing of breastfeeding with respect to the administration times is of little benefit. Although the dose in milk is low, metformin is sometimes detectable in low levels in the serum of breastfed infants. One sizeable prospective study found no adverse effects in breastfed infants. Metformin is sometimes used as a galactogogue in women with reduced milk supply, but there is no evidence that it is effective. Metformin should be used with caution while nursing newborn and premature infants and those with renal impairment.","truncated":false,"source_section":"Summary of Use during Lactation","notice":"Preserve relevant source context when describing adverse findings; this is source text, not a personal treatment plan."},"alternatives_policy":{"handling":"excluded","present_in_parsed_source":true,"notice":"Alternate Drugs to Consider is excluded from returned text and synthesis. Consult the linked LactMed source; this tool does not recommend substitutions."},"population_context":{"source_population":"lactation","pregnancy_evidence":false},"continuity_of_care_notice":"Stopping or changing a medication can also carry risks. Discuss changes with the prescribing clinician. This tool cannot advise starting, stopping, or changing treatment.","references_total_in_record":10,"citation_instructions":"Numbers in square brackets, such as [54], are LactMed's own citations. Link each finding you report to its study using the references list (PubMed URL